<p>A <strong>relational database</strong> stores an application’s data in tables and lets the application ask questions across them. A shop can keep its customers, products and orders, then ask which orders a customer placed, what each contained and how much is still due. The same stored facts serve the checkout, the customer’s order history and the shop’s reports.</p>

<p>Storing those facts is only part of the job. An order should belong to a customer who exists. Its quantities should be positive. Raising a product’s price should leave earlier orders at the price their buyers agreed to. Some of these rules can be declared in the database itself, so every application writing to it has to obey them. Others depend on how the application makes its changes.</p>

<p>Relational databases give you three tools for this work: tables that distinguish the facts you need to keep, SQL for reading and changing them, and transactions for making several changes succeed or fail together. This collection follows those tools through an invented shop selling tea and tea things, from its first tables to running the database behind a busy application.</p>

<section aria-labelledby="tables-and-keys">
  <h2 id="tables-and-keys">Tables, keys and the facts they hold</h2>

  <p>A <strong>table</strong> holds facts of one kind. Each <strong>row</strong> is one record, and each <strong>column</strong> is a named part of that record. In <code>customers</code>, one row stands for one customer. Its <code>customer_id</code> identifies the customer and its <code>name</code> gives their name. Each column also has a type: a customer identifier here is a whole number, and a name is text.</p>

  <p>The shop keeps orders in another table. Find order 103 below, then follow its <code>customer_id</code> to the matching customer.</p>

  <OrderTables part="customers" />

  <p>Order 103 belongs to customer 1, Mina Shah. So does order 101. The name is stored once in <code>customers</code>, while each order records which customer placed it. Tariq’s order holds 2 instead, and Edda has a customer row without any orders. A customer can exist before they buy anything.</p>

  <p>The shop declares <code>customer_id</code> as the <strong>primary key</strong> of <code>customers</code>: a value that identifies exactly one row, with no duplicates or missing values. <code>order_id</code> does the same for <code>orders</code>. A name would be a poor identifier, because two customers can share a name and a customer can change theirs. The identifier lets orders keep referring to the same customer.</p>

  <p>The <code>customer_id</code> in an order is a <strong>foreign key</strong>. It refers to a key in another table. The shop declares that each order must have a customer identifier matching a row in <code>customers</code>; the database can then refuse an order for a customer who does not exist. Giving two columns the same name would not establish that rule on its own.</p>

  <p>An order can contain several products, so its contents need rows of their own. The shop calls each one an <strong>order line</strong> and stores them in <code>order_items</code>. Below are the two lines of order 103 and the products they refer to.</p>

  <OrderTables part="items" />

  <p>Line 1 says that order 103 contains one of product P7, the blue mug, at 20.00 each. Line 2 says it contains two of P3, the tea, at 4.50 each. That makes 29.00 for the order: 20.00 plus twice 4.50. The product identifiers connect each line to its product, just as the customer identifier connected an order to its customer.</p>

  <p>Here the primary key takes two columns together: <code>order_id</code> and <code>line_no</code>. Line 1 of order 103 is a different row from line 1 of order 102. The line number alone is not enough to identify it. The database refuses a second line 1 within order 103, while allowing another order to have its own line 1.</p>

  <p>The two price columns happen to agree, but they answer different questions. <code>products.price</code> is what the shop offers the product for now. <code>order_items.unit_price</code> is what the buyer agreed to pay for each unit on that order line. Suppose the shop raises the mug’s offered price to 22.00. Changing that product row leaves order 103’s mug line at 20.00 and its total at 29.00. Calculating the old order from the product’s new price would instead give 31.00, rewriting what Mina agreed to.</p>

  <p>Keeping both prices is therefore deliberate. The product row describes the offer; the order line records the sale. The database does not infer those meanings or automatically protect an agreed price from later edits. The design gives the application somewhere to keep each fact, and the application has to use it correctly.</p>

  <p>The rules declared with these tables are called <strong>constraints</strong>. Keys are two kinds. A <strong>check constraint</strong> tests a condition, such as requiring each order line’s quantity to be greater than zero. With these constraints in place, the database refuses changes such as these:</p>

  <ResultTable
    caption="Changes the shop’s declared constraints refuse"
    columns={['Attempted change', 'Why it is refused']}
    rows={[
      ['Add another customer with customer_id 1', 'The primary key already identifies Mina.'],
      ['Give an order customer_id 99', 'The foreign key has no matching customer.'],
      ['Give an order line a quantity of 0', 'The quantity must be greater than zero.'],
    ]}
  />

  <p>These rules catch a duplicate identifier, a broken reference or an impossible quantity whichever application attempts the change. They do not tell the database how to place an order. Choosing the tables and their rules is data design; asking for Mina’s orders is SQL; saving an order and reducing stock together is transaction work. Each starts from the same facts and identifiers.</p>
</section>

